Now that your frontend is completely locked down, moving to the backend DTO validation is the perfect next step. A golden rule of system design is: **Never trust the client.** Even with the best React validation, someone could bypass the UI and hit your API via Postman.
To enforce these exact rules in Spring Boot, we will use jakarta.validation.constraints.* annotations (or javax if you are on Spring Boot 2.x). We will also use a clever Spring Boot trick (@AssertTrue) to handle that complex dynamic currency decimal logic!
Here is how to set up your DTOs and Controller.
### 1. The Parent Batch DTO
Create a DTO to catch the entire JSON payload. This validates the common remarks and ensures the row list isn't empty.
```java
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotEmpty;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Pattern;
import java.util.List;

public class JournalBatchRequestDto {

    // Allows empty string, OR exactly 2-30 alphanumeric characters
    @Pattern(regexp = "^$|^[a-zA-Z0-9]{2,30}$", message = "Common Batch Remarks must be empty or between 2 and 30 alphanumeric characters")
    private String commonBatchRemarks;

    // Ensures the list exists and has at least one item
    @NotEmpty(message = "Batch must contain at least one journal row")
    @Valid // CRITICAL: This tells Spring to dive inside the list and validate each row individually!
    private List<JournalRowDto> rows;

    // Getters and Setters
    public String getCommonBatchRemarks() { return commonBatchRemarks; }
    public void setCommonBatchRemarks(String commonBatchRemarks) { this commonBatchRemarks = commonBatchRemarks; }
    public List<JournalRowDto> getRows() { return rows; }
    public void setRows(List<JournalRowDto> rows) { this.rows = rows; }
}

```
### 2. The Individual Row DTO
This is where the heavy lifting happens. Notice how we allow an optional - (minus sign) in the amount regex, because your frontend converts "Credit" into negative numbers before submitting!
```java
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Pattern;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.AssertTrue;

public class JournalRowDto {

    @NotBlank(message = "Branch is mandatory")
    private String branch;

    @NotBlank(message = "Currency is mandatory")
    private String currency;

    @NotBlank(message = "CGL is mandatory")
    private String cgl;

    // Allows optional '-', up to 16 digits, and optional decimal up to 4 places
    @NotBlank(message = "Amount is mandatory")
    @Pattern(regexp = "^-?\\d{1,16}(\\.\\d{1,4})?$", message = "Amount must be a valid number with up to 16 digits and a maximum of 4 decimal places")
    private String amount;

    // Optional field, so we just restrict it to 0-8 digits
    @Pattern(regexp = "^\\d{0,8}$", message = "Product Code must be a maximum of 8 digits")
    private String productType;

    @NotBlank(message = "Remarks are mandatory")
    @Pattern(regexp = "^[a-zA-Z0-9]{2,30}$", message = "Remarks must be between 2 and 30 alphanumeric characters")
    private String remarks;

    @NotBlank(message = "AR Flag is mandatory")
    @Pattern(regexp = "^A$", message = "AR Flag must be exactly 'A'")
    private String arFlag;

    // --- CROSS-FIELD VALIDATION (The Currency/Decimal Rule) ---
    // Spring Boot will automatically run any method annotated with @AssertTrue during validation
    @AssertTrue(message = "INR currency allows a maximum of 2 decimal places. Other currencies allow up to 4.")
    public boolean isAmountDecimalValid() {
        if (amount == null || currency == null) return true; // Skip if null, let @NotBlank handle the error
        
        String[] parts = amount.replace("-", "").split("\\.");
        int fractionLength = parts.length > 1 ? parts[1].length() : 0;

        if ("INR".equalsIgnoreCase(currency)) {
            return fractionLength <= 2;
        } else {
            return fractionLength <= 4;
        }
    }

    // Getters and Setters...
}

```
### 3. Activating it in your Controller
To make Spring Boot actually execute these annotations, you **must** add the @Valid annotation next to the @RequestBody in your JournalRequestController.
```java
