### 4. Handling the Errors Gracefully (Global Exception Handler)
If a validation fails, Spring Boot throws a MethodArgumentNotValidException. By default, this returns a massive, ugly stack trace to the frontend.
If you don't already have a @RestControllerAdvice class in your project, add this one. It catches the validation errors and formats them cleanly so your frontend useCustomSnackbar can easily display them!
```java
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.validation.FieldError;
import org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ResponseStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalValidationExceptionHandler {

    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST)
    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public Map<String, String> handleValidationExceptions(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        Map<String, String> errors = new HashMap<>();
        
        ex.getBindingResult().getAllErrors().forEach((error) -> {
            String fieldName = ((FieldError) error).getField();
            String errorMessage = error.getDefaultMessage();
            errors.put(fieldName, errorMessage);
        });
        
        return errors;
    }
}

```
With this setup, your Java backend is now a fortress. If Postman sends a product code with 9 digits, or an INR amount with 3 decimal places, Spring Boot will instantly reject it with a clean 400 Bad Request and your custom error message!
